enchilada: cpufreq governor (sweetcpufreq=2)

sweetcpufreq=1 pins the clusters at their fastest state forever: smooth, but hot and hungry when idle. sweetcpufreq=2 starts a kernel thread once all CPUs are up (0311's second sweet_cpu_clocks() call) that every 20 ms takes each cluster's busiest CPU's non-idle share (recount idle time through a new sweet_cpu_idle_abs() in osfmk/kern/processor.c; PROCESSOR_CPU_LOAD_INFO only has 10 ms ticks) and picks the slowest full-core OSM state at or above cur * util / 0.8, the fastest above 80 % busy, stepping down at most 2 states after 3 windows in a row below. L3 follows the faster cluster. Touch reports (sweet_touch.c) call sweet_cpufreq_boost(), which holds all domains at their fastest for 250 ms. The OSM bases are now mapped once and kept; the perf-state register is written only on a change. One line every 10 s gives each domain's time share per MHz. Without the accounting or with a domain disabled, =2 stays at the fastest state as =1 does; =1 and 0/absent are unchanged.

--- a/osfmk/kern/processor.c
+++ b/osfmk/kern/processor.c
@@ -663,6 +663,25 @@
 	ticks[CPU_STATE_IDLE] += (uint32_t)(idle_time / hz_tick_interval);
 }
 
+/*
+ * 0323: a CPU's total idle time in mach absolute units (recount, up to now if it idles now), for
+ * the enchilada cpufreq governor in IOKit, which cannot see struct processor. The tick-based
+ * PROCESSOR_CPU_LOAD_INFO above is too coarse for its 20 ms windows. UINT64_MAX: no such CPU.
+ */
+uint64_t sweet_cpu_idle_abs(int cpu);
+uint64_t
+sweet_cpu_idle_abs(int cpu)
+{
+	struct recount_usage usage = { 0 };
+	uint64_t idle_time = 0;
+
+	if (cpu < 0 || cpu >= MAX_SCHED_CPUS || processor_array[cpu] == PROCESSOR_NULL) {
+		return UINT64_MAX;
+	}
+	recount_processor_usage(&processor_array[cpu]->pr_recount, &usage, &idle_time);
+	return idle_time;
+}
+
 kern_return_t
 processor_info(
 	processor_t     processor,
